      "text": "On this day in 1942, a Nazi spy team landed on a Long Island, NY beach in a rubber boat. Their mission? Destroy U.S. infrastructure on the East Coast to sabotage the war effort. \n \nTheir mission failed. While patrolling the beach, Coast Guard Boatswain’s Mate Second Class John “Jack” Cullen spotted the team making their way ashore. After a tense confrontation, Cullen ran back to report the incident to his commanding officer. The Nazis were caught by the @FBI  within two weeks.\n\nBM2 Cullen’s legacy continues today. Exhibiting the same vigilance, Coast Guard law enforcement teams have been conducting shoreside patrols in southern California, ensuring the security of our borders and maritime approaches. Then and now, America’s investment in the Coast Guard enhances national security and strengthens our unique capabilities to protect the American people.\n\nRead more here: https://t.co/hDQCzkUPp8\n\n#USCG #WWII #lawenforcement #maritimesecurity #coastguard \n@DHSgov",
      "text_resolved": "On this day in 1942, a Nazi spy team landed on a Long Island, NY beach in a rubber boat. Their mission? Destroy U.S. infrastructure on the East Coast to sabotage the war effort. \n \nTheir mission failed. While patrolling the beach, Coast Guard Boatswain’s Mate Second Class John “Jack” Cullen spotted the team making their way ashore. After a tense confrontation, Cullen ran back to report the incident to his commanding officer. The Nazis were caught by the @FBI  within two weeks.\n\nBM2 Cullen’s legacy continues today. Exhibiting the same vigilance, Coast Guard law enforcement teams have been conducting shoreside patrols in southern California, ensuring the security of our borders and maritime approaches. Then and now, America’s investment in the Coast Guard enhances national security and strengthens our unique capabilities to protect the American people.\n\nRead more here: https://www.mycg.uscg.mil/News/Article/4216202/bags-of-clams-not-likely-heres-how-a-coast-guard-seaman-recruit-outwitted-four/\n\n#USCG #WWII #lawenforcement #maritimesecurity #coastguard \n@DHSgov",
